00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:27.420
Like a jockstrap?

571
00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:28.080
I don't know.

572
00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:29.580
I think like jock, like athlete.

573
00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:30.540
Oh, like an athlete.

574
00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:31.500
Jock to Vaughn.

575
00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:33.180
There's no V in it, lady.

576
00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:34.160
It's joctagon.

577
00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:35.580
Oh, joctagon.

578
00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:36.060
Yeah.

579
00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:38.240
Did I say Vaughn every time?

580
00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:39.620
That last time you did.

581
00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:41.080
Yeah, I did the last time.

582
00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:43.020
Joctagon, like octagon.

583
00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:43.480
Mm-hmm.

584
00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:43.920
Yeah.

585
00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:45.500
I like Champlify.

586
00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:50.180
That, to me, would have hit the joke harder than athlete, but maybe it's too ridiculous.

587
00:26:50.540 --> 00:26:52.500
Well, athlete is a better name than Champlify.

588
00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:53.520
Yeah.

589
00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:55.640
But Champlify is very funny to say.

590
00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:56.360
It is.

591
00:26:56.880 --> 00:27:00.020
Lady, I have a tangent that just popped into my head.

592
00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.220
It is in no way related to this episode.

593
00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:04.920
In fact, it's related to an episode of The Crown.

594
00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:05.920
Oh, I'm taking—

595
00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:06.100
Do you mind?

596
00:27:06.220 --> 00:27:07.420
I don't—are you kidding?

597
00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:07.920
The Crown?

598
00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:08.640
I don't mind.

599
00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:09.760
I'm taking off my glasses.

600
00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:10.880
I'm getting my cup of tea.

601
00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:11.520
Let's hear it.

602
00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:13.180
Okay, this is totally unplanned.

603
00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:13.560
Okay.

604
00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:15.100
It's not in my document.

605
00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:16.140
I know it's not.

606
00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:16.580
Okay.

607
00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:22.180
The other night, Lee and I were watching The Crown, the latest season, the final season.

608
00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:22.500
Yeah.

609
00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:25.980
And I'm on the episode where Prince William has gone to college.

610
00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:26.500
Yes.

611
00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:29.520
And he goes out to a party one night, and they do beer bongs.

612
00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:30.180
Yeah.

613
00:27:30.680 --> 00:27:32.240
Have you ever done a beer bong?

614
00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:33.580
Because I haven't.

615
00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:36.840
And as I was watching it, I was like, who wants to do that?

616
00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:38.560
It looks awful.

617
00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:40.540
It looks like torture.

618
00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:42.180
No, I've never done it.

619
00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:44.900
The closest I've come to that is in college.

620
00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:47.760
I played on a co-ed softball team.

621
00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:48.140
Uh-huh.

622
00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:52.100
And at second base, they had a pony keg.

623
00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:52.740
Uh-huh.

624
00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:56.000
And if you made it to second base, you could do a keg stand.

625
00:27:56.360 --> 00:27:57.340
What's a keg stand?

626
00:27:57.580 --> 00:27:58.920
You do a handstand.

627
00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:00.080
Oh, this is worse.

628
00:28:00.260 --> 00:28:01.080
This is getting worse.

629
00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.540
A pair of a keg, and then there's a little hose, and they put beer.

630
00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:05.160
You have to drink upside down?

631
00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:05.480
Yeah.

632
00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:06.660
I did a keg stand.

633
00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:08.720
Anyway, I've done that.

634
00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:13.780
But it just—basically, it just goes all over your face, and, you know, you're just like,

635
00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:14.800
this is stupid.

636
00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:16.080
But you're—I was in college.

637
00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:18.100
I have not done—what's it called?

638
00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:18.600
A beer pong?

639
00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:19.220
Beer bong.

640
00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:19.840
Oh, bong.

641
00:28:20.120 --> 00:28:20.660
A beer pong.

642
00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:21.580
Beer bong.

643
00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:21.960
Bong.

644
00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:24.340
Why would I think it was beer pong?

645
00:28:24.620 --> 00:28:26.220
Well, there's a game called Beer Pong.

646
00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:26.500
Oh.

647
00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:31.040
Where you try to get a ping pong ball into a thing of beer, and then you have to chug it.

648
00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:35.940
I did that, but on a TV show, and they were like, Angela, we want you to toss this ping

649
00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:40.760
pong ball into the cup of beer, and they were like, we'll roll a few because you probably

650
00:28:40.760 --> 00:28:42.020
won't get it, you know, right away.

651
00:28:42.140 --> 00:28:43.140
I got it on the first take.

652
00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:43.960
Was it real beer?

653
00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:45.700
Was it like a reality thing?

654
00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:46.160
No, it was fakie.

655
00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:48.020
Oh, it was for a scripted show.

656
00:28:48.020 --> 00:28:50.500
Yeah, it was the show I did with Kate Walsh, Bad Judge.

657
00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:50.920
Okay.

658
00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:53.900
But I made the ping pong in the red solo cup on my first try.

659
00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:55.440
I think I could be good at it.

660
00:28:55.600 --> 00:29:01.520
So wait, when you play beer pong, you throw the ball, and you're trying to get it in the

661
00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:03.300
cup so that you can chug it?

662
00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:04.580
No, the other person.

663
00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:06.140
Or the other person has to chug it?

664
00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:07.560
I think it's the other person.

665
00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:08.280
Right.

666
00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:12.580
Because, like, if I were playing that, I would just always miss because I would never want

667
00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:13.280
to chug the beer.

668
00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:17.040
Yeah, so I fake played beer pong, but never played it in real life.

669
00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:22.260
I have done a keg stand playing softball, but I've never done a beer pong.

670
00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:24.000
It looks difficult.

671
00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:30.280
When I was a freshman in college, like, the first two weeks of school, me and some of the

672
00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:34.580
other gals on my floor at my dorm, we got invited to a frat party.

673
00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:35.380
Oh, gosh.

674
00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:41.080
I went to it for less than an hour and never went to another one again.

675
00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:41.380
Yeah.

676
00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:43.500
My entire—I was like, I don't get it.

677
00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:43.780
Yeah.

678
00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:45.180
This is not for me.

679
00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:49.740
I am confused by all of the activities.

680
00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:51.520
None of them seem fun.

681
00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:54.260
I am very anxious at this event.

682
00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:54.560
Yeah.

683
00:29:55.040 --> 00:30:01.060
And then later, when I was an upperclassman, like, there was a group of guys in the theater

684
00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:03.580
department who had a theater fraternity.

685
00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:04.160
Okay.

686
00:30:04.580 --> 00:30:07.400
And I was like, maybe that's my jam.

687
00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:07.840
Right.

688
00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:08.700
I know all these guys.

689
00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:10.220
I'm doing plays with these guys.

690
00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:10.640
Yeah.

691
00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:14.280
And you didn't have to be in the theater department to be in that fraternity, but it

692
00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:15.260
was sort of like you were.

693
00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:19.200
I went to that one, and I was like, I don't know what's happened to all of these people

694
00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:24.440
who I adore while we're doing theater, but now I can't—it's a culture that I—

695
00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:24.660
Yeah.

696
00:30:25.060 --> 00:30:27.240
I just—I don't know what's happening here.

697
00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:32.360
But I did—so I'm watching The Crown, and I'm watching the beer bonging.

698
00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:33.040
Okay.

699
00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:41.260
And I was like, I've never done—there's nothing from the time I was born in my DNA that

700
00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:42.840
could get myself to do this activity.

701
00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:43.380
Yeah, no.

702
00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:48.380
And it's very popular, and many people do it, and I guess are enjoying it and having

703
00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:50.620
fun, but it—I can't.

704
00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:51.800
I don't get it.

705
00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:53.680
I don't, like, get it.

706
00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:56.320
I've never done a beer bong.

707
00:30:56.740 --> 00:30:57.820
I've had no desire.

708
00:30:57.980 --> 00:30:59.340
I've seen people do them around me.

709
00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:05.440
My college didn't have sorority and fraternity houses, so a lot of that, like, kind of big

710
00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:07.460
frat party thing wasn't part of my college.

711
00:31:07.660 --> 00:31:12.220
But I do notice that, like, if I do have a story, it's around, like, a sport.

712
00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:14.780
It's like softball or a football game.

713
00:31:14.980 --> 00:31:17.200
But I don't like chugging stuff.

714
00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:22.060
I don't like speeding up the getting drunk process, right?

715
00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:26.800
Like, I don't—I haven't gone out for the purpose of getting drunk super faSt.

716
00:31:27.020 --> 00:31:29.760
I probably shouldn't have told you I brought shots for us today.

717
00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:30.880
I'm kidding.

718
00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:35.800
Like, shots, shots, shots, shots, shots.

719
00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:36.660
No.

720
00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:37.200
Okay.

721
00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:38.940
Sorry for that tangent.

722
00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:40.800
It just popped into my head.

723
00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:42.560
I doubled down on your tangent.

724
00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:46.660
I could have just said, no, I've never done one, and we'd be done with this conversation.

725
00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:51.440
Well, let me tell you what I would love to do as an activity at a party is build a giant

726
00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:52.160
card wall.

727
00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:53.560
I would, too.

728
00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:54.940
I would love that.

729
00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:55.880
That's my jam.

730
00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:57.160
They're really hard to do.

731
00:31:57.160 --> 00:32:00.420
I've tried to do it with a deck of cards before, and they're real slick.

732
00:32:00.620 --> 00:32:02.140
You need an old deck.

733
00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:03.940
What?

734
00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:10.790
I don't know, lady.

735
00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:12.870
I don't know that just the—

736
00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:16.770
It's just the—

737
00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:25.090
Just, like, the earnestness and authority with which you have given us this tip on how

738
00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:27.410
to build a successful card tower.

739
00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:31.810
You need an old deck.

740
00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:33.810
They can't be shiny or slick.

741
00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:41.150
I noted that Pete used these complaint cards, and I looked at them, and they are like the

742
00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:42.690
rougher cardstock.

743
00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:44.190
They're not the slick paper.

744
00:32:44.190 --> 00:32:45.130
I'm just saying.

745
00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:47.530
More successful tower.

746
00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:48.350
That's right.

747
00:32:48.650 --> 00:32:52.270
Well, let's tell you why Pete is going to start a tower.

748
00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:57.570
He's not starting it yet, but he has a talking head where he says he's been tasked with entering

749
00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:02.910
customer complaints into a computer, and then after that, he has to fill out a comment card.

750
00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:06.350
He asked Andy, why do I have to do this?

751
00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:09.690
They're already in the computer, and Andy said, chillax.

752
00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:11.890
And then went on a big boat ride.

753
00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:12.430
Yes.

754
00:33:13.110 --> 00:33:17.370
So he's decided that he's going to use the cards, he's going to give them purpose,

755
00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:19.830
and he's going to build a paper tower.

756
00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:20.350
Yeah.

757
00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:25.310
He says, don't give me a pointless office drawer, because I will build a little paper

758
00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:25.730
house.

759
00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:26.550
Fight the power.

760
00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:27.970
I love him.

761
00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:28.650
