00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:31.560
It's so crazy.

1021
00:43:31.640 --> 00:43:31.960
I know.

1022
00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:32.660
I know.

1023
00:43:33.620 --> 00:43:38.320
Dwight says, listen, there are different ways to get revenge, like defecating in a paper bag.

1024
00:43:38.500 --> 00:43:43.160
Trevor quickly says, I've been on the receiving end of that a few times, and it's devastating.

1025
00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:46.440
That's another line that made me laugh.

1026
00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:52.420
What they decide on is that Trevor is going to damage Oscar's kneecaps.

1027
00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:53.620
Yeah, and kneecapping.

1028
00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:54.040
Yeah.

1029
00:43:54.200 --> 00:43:55.300
Hit him in the kneecaps.

1030
00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:57.020
It's going to happen at 4 p.m.

1031
00:43:57.660 --> 00:43:57.820
Yes.

1032
00:43:57.820 --> 00:43:59.520
No turning back once Trevor leaves.

1033
00:43:59.680 --> 00:44:00.240
That's right.

1034
00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:02.600
Fan question from Jenna F.

1035
00:44:02.640 --> 00:44:03.540
in Los Angeles.

1036
00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:09.440
Angela, Chris talked about being in the van all together with you guys shooting these scenes.

1037
00:44:09.740 --> 00:44:11.440
Can you please share about doing these?

1038
00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:12.600
These were so great.

1039
00:44:13.240 --> 00:44:16.120
I mean, how did you get through these?

1040
00:44:16.380 --> 00:44:20.380
Almost everything he said made me laugh because he said it with such earnestness.

1041
00:44:20.940 --> 00:44:25.100
This character Trevor is such like a funny guy.

1042
00:44:25.360 --> 00:44:30.140
But there was one moment that really got me, and it's actually in deleted scenes.

1043
00:44:30.140 --> 00:44:33.020
I'm not going to play it, Jenna, because it's more of a sight gag.

1044
00:44:33.160 --> 00:44:33.840
Oh, okay.

1045
00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:35.820
But I have to share it in stories.

1046
00:44:35.820 --> 00:44:36.580
I have to.

1047
00:44:36.580 --> 00:44:42.300
So there's this very serious moment in the van, and Angela Martin says to Trevor, what

1048
00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:43.520
do you do if you get caught?

1049
00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:46.240
And then he says, I slipped through people's fingers.

1050
00:44:46.660 --> 00:44:51.600
And then Dwight tries to grab him, and he does this hand move, and Dwight's like, I can't

1051
00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:51.880
get him.

1052
00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:52.740
I can't get him.

1053
00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:55.840
And then totally improvised, Rain's like, you go.

1054
00:44:56.040 --> 00:44:56.880
You try, Angela.

1055
00:44:57.180 --> 00:45:02.740
So then I just start trying to grab him, and Chris as Trevor just keeps doing these moves,

1056
00:45:02.820 --> 00:45:04.480
and then I'm like, I can't get him.

1057
00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:12.680
Cracking up at how ridiculous me and Rain trying to grab Chris and him doing these ridiculous

1058
00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:14.940
hand moves, and he's like, slips through your fingers.

1059
00:45:15.160 --> 00:45:15.960
I can't get me.

1060
00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:20.680
It was just such silly physical gags, but we just lost it.

1061
00:45:21.020 --> 00:45:21.880
Oh, my gosh.

1062
00:45:21.880 --> 00:45:23.120
I wish that was in the episode.

1063
00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:24.200
That sounds so funny.

1064
00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:26.000
Imagine Angela trying.

1065
00:45:26.160 --> 00:45:27.100
We were like tussling.

1066
00:45:27.420 --> 00:45:33.260
Like, I'm trying to grab him, and he's like swatting at me, but we're seated in a van.

1067
00:45:33.440 --> 00:45:34.660
I mean, amazing.

1068
00:45:35.060 --> 00:45:35.540
Amazing.

1069
00:45:35.540 --> 00:45:36.980
It was such a fun day.

1070
00:45:37.600 --> 00:45:44.040
Well, there was another thing that Chris shared with us that I found so relatable and

1071
00:45:44.040 --> 00:45:44.660
so charming.

1072
00:45:44.660 --> 00:45:48.400
This was about just a memory he had of being on The Office.

1073
00:45:49.080 --> 00:45:50.060
Here's what he said.

1074
00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:57.140
Another thing I remember is that The Office got me glasses to my actual prescription, and

1075
00:45:57.140 --> 00:46:02.360
I think in my entire career of acting, that's the only time that a set has gone above and

1076
00:46:02.360 --> 00:46:02.860
beyond like that.

1077
00:46:02.860 --> 00:46:04.880
It's really, really thoughtful to do.

1078
00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:11.040
I get a lot of parts as guys in glasses, like weird guys in glasses.

1079
00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:13.040
And sometimes they make me wear my own glasses.

1080
00:46:13.040 --> 00:46:17.840
And then a lot of times they want you to wear like slightly weirder glasses, and that just

1081
00:46:17.840 --> 00:46:18.740
means I'm blind.

1082
00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:20.560
It means on a lot of sets.

1083
00:46:20.760 --> 00:46:24.720
Like, honestly, anytime you've ever seen me acting, if I'm wearing glasses that aren't

1084
00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:26.600
mine, I'm just blind.

1085
00:46:26.600 --> 00:46:28.180
Except The Office.

1086
00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:34.160
The Office contacted me ahead of time and got my prescription and had a few sets of glasses

1087
00:46:34.160 --> 00:46:35.220
made with my prescription.

1088
00:46:35.220 --> 00:46:40.360
So I'm wearing these big goofy glasses in the show, and they got my actual prescription.

1089
00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:45.740
And I can tell you, that's above and beyond and extraordinarily thoughtful because I don't,

1090
00:46:45.740 --> 00:46:50.520
when someone calls cut and there's a million moving parts and you're just a guest star and

1091
00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:53.160
you have to be like, can somebody find the PA who took my glasses?

1092
00:46:53.180 --> 00:46:57.600
Because you literally can't walk 10 feet without banging into something or like knocking into

1093
00:46:57.600 --> 00:46:58.640
a piece of equipment without it.

1094
00:46:58.720 --> 00:47:02.280
Like, you do feel like really stranded out there on a set.

1095
00:47:02.280 --> 00:47:08.600
So the fact that The Office got my prescription and made those glasses real was shocking and

1096
00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:10.620
really cool and thoughtful and nice.

1097
00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:13.640
Well, Angela, we know who that is.

1098
00:47:13.800 --> 00:47:14.980
We know who did that.

1099
00:47:15.460 --> 00:47:15.740
Phil.

1100
00:47:16.040 --> 00:47:16.660
That's Phil.

1101
00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:17.080
Yeah.

1102
00:47:17.380 --> 00:47:18.600
And I'm going to get choked up.

1103
00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:19.280
I know.

1104
00:47:19.440 --> 00:47:19.840
Because.

1105
00:47:20.060 --> 00:47:20.680
I know.

1106
00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:22.160
You know, we lost Phil last year.

1107
00:47:22.320 --> 00:47:23.040
He passed away.

1108
00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:26.140
And yet, as we do this rewatch, I just continue.

1109
00:47:26.460 --> 00:47:27.460
To think of him.

1110
00:47:27.680 --> 00:47:27.920
Yeah.

1111
00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:28.680
Yeah, me too.

1112
00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:32.380
And with this story and with the card tower, that was Phil.

1113
00:47:32.640 --> 00:47:32.820
Yeah.

1114
00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:33.920
You know, and.

1115
00:47:34.420 --> 00:47:36.520
The sandwich with the pipe in it.

1116
00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:37.520
It's coming up.

1117
00:47:37.740 --> 00:47:38.260
That's Phil.

1118
00:47:38.820 --> 00:47:44.820
But you know, Ange, when I was on Splitting Up Together, they wanted my character to sometimes

1119
00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:46.920
wear glasses, but not my glasses.

1120
00:47:47.400 --> 00:47:52.960
And originally, they just gave me some glasses with glass in them.

1121
00:47:53.160 --> 00:47:57.300
And every time we would finish a scene, I would have to be like,

1122
00:47:57.300 --> 00:47:59.100
do you know who has my real glasses?

1123
00:47:59.520 --> 00:48:01.140
Do we know where my real glasses are?

1124
00:48:01.280 --> 00:48:04.120
And I ended up losing my real glasses several times.

1125
00:48:04.440 --> 00:48:04.500
Oh, no.

1126
00:48:04.560 --> 00:48:09.100
Because I would take them off and put them down on a shelf on set and then put my fakie

1127
00:48:09.100 --> 00:48:09.780
glasses on.

1128
00:48:10.020 --> 00:48:16.820
And so in the second season, they had my, quote unquote, show glasses made with my prescription.

1129
00:48:17.340 --> 00:48:22.420
And it changed everything because then I could read my script in between scenes.

1130
00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:27.980
And so when Chris told this story, I did also think of that like, yes, yes, Chris, I've been

1131
00:48:27.980 --> 00:48:28.480
there too.

1132
00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:30.040
I know what that feels like.

1133
00:48:30.260 --> 00:48:33.360
But how, to his point, lovely that they did it for a guest star.

1134
00:48:33.480 --> 00:48:33.820
I know.

1135
00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:35.060
That was your show.

1136
00:48:35.240 --> 00:48:35.560
I know.

1137
00:48:35.560 --> 00:48:37.900
You were a series regular and you didn't have glasses.

1138
00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:38.800
Till season two.

1139
00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:39.220
Yeah.

1140
00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:43.400
It was just one of those things where it was like, hey, we just realized we could put

1141
00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:44.800
my prescription in these glasses.

1142
00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:45.920
Everyone's like, oh, yeah.

1143
00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:46.460
Oh, yeah.

1144
00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:47.540
Now you can see.

1145
00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:49.220
But, you know, Phil being Phil.

1146
00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:50.200
He thought of it.

1147
00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:50.980
Thought of it right away.

1148
00:48:51.140 --> 00:48:51.300
Yep.

1149
00:48:51.800 --> 00:48:55.480
Should we go to crazy lunch with Jim and Stanley and Phyllis?

1150
00:48:55.720 --> 00:48:56.560
Oh, my gosh.

1151
00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:57.860
This is hilarious.

1152
00:48:58.280 --> 00:49:01.860
Talk about, at the top of their game, Leslie and Phyllis in this scene.

1153
00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:04.880
First of all, Stanley is in no rush to talk.

1154
00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:08.360
And he just wants to order surf and turf with an extra side of surf.

1155
00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:10.820
They're going to really run up this bill.

1156
00:49:10.960 --> 00:49:12.920
Phyllis wants to know how much wine they have.

1157
00:49:13.120 --> 00:49:14.600
Not what kind of wine.

1158
00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:15.540
How much?

1159
00:49:15.880 --> 00:49:16.180
Yes.

1160
00:49:16.580 --> 00:49:17.720
Well, location alert.

1161
00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:20.800
We shot this at Delmonico's on Ventura Boulevard.

1162
00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:23.660
I think this is the same place that Michael ordered his gabagool.

1163
00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:24.400
Oh.

1164
00:49:24.620 --> 00:49:25.240
Same restaurant.

1165
00:49:25.480 --> 00:49:29.200
And the waiter was played by guest star Noah Blake.

1166
00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:33.580
And I think what this scene tells us is that Jim has an uphill battle here.

1167
00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:34.100
Yeah.

1168
00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:35.860
It's going to be a long lunch.

1169
00:49:35.980 --> 00:49:36.360
It is.

1170
00:49:37.040 --> 00:49:39.160
Oscar now brings Angela a cookie.

1171
00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:41.180
And she calls him an angel.

1172
00:49:41.660 --> 00:49:42.060
All right.

1173
00:49:42.220 --> 00:49:43.040
Come on, Oscar.

1174
00:49:43.600 --> 00:49:44.200
Fine.

1175
00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:48.380
I get it that maybe her complaining about the thermostat didn't tip you off.

1176
00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:51.560
But Angela just called you an angel.

1177
00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:53.040
Come on.

1178
00:49:53.040 --> 00:49:53.500
Yes.

1179
00:49:53.780 --> 00:50:00.000
I think normally if you handed Angela a cookie that large, she would comment on it, right?

1180
00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:00.440
Yeah.

1181
00:50:00.500 --> 00:50:01.680
She'd be like, thank you.

1182
00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:02.600
Why is it so big?

1183
00:50:03.040 --> 00:50:05.360
Or she'd say, why are you giving me a cookie?

1184
00:50:05.540 --> 00:50:06.700
You never give me a cookie.

1185
00:50:06.880 --> 00:50:07.780
I don't want this.

1186
00:50:08.620 --> 00:50:09.100
Exactly.

1187
00:50:09.500 --> 00:50:11.540
When did they start making cookies like this?

1188
00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:12.560
I don't like it.

1189
00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:13.500
It has nuts.

1190
00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:14.500
There'd be something.

1191
00:50:14.960 --> 00:50:15.580
There'd be something.

1192
00:50:15.840 --> 00:50:17.300
But she calls him an angel.

1193
00:50:17.300 --> 00:50:21.460
And as he walks away, she crushes the cookies into her hands.

1194
00:50:21.740 --> 00:50:23.100
Which Dwight notices.

1195
00:50:23.660 --> 00:50:24.120
Yes.

1196
00:50:24.280 --> 00:50:25.940
He's putting all the pieces together.

1197
00:50:25.940 --> 00:50:31.000
Because up until this point, he didn't know who this person was that Trevor had to take out.

1198
00:50:31.420 --> 00:50:33.200
Well, Angela, we got a fan question.

1199
00:50:33.700 --> 00:50:36.640
People want to know, how many cookies did you have to smash?

1200
00:50:36.640 --> 00:50:37.260
Do you remember?

1201
00:50:37.780 --> 00:50:38.560
I don't remember.

1202
00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:40.660
But it was, I think we did more than one take.

1203
00:50:40.700 --> 00:50:42.580
So it was probably three or so.

1204
00:50:42.780 --> 00:50:45.560
I'm sure Phil had a whole basket of them standing by.

1205
00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:46.580
Exactly.

1206
00:50:47.200 --> 00:50:52.740
And the one thing they wanted is for me to crush it and for it to just kind of fall off my hands into the trash.

1207
00:50:53.720 --> 00:50:55.140
That was all scripted.

1208
00:50:55.480 --> 00:51:04.300
And that was, I would say, of all the scenes that I had to do for this episode, that was the hardest one for me to ground in reality.

1209
00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:06.440
Crumbling the cookie.

1210
00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:07.060
Yes.

1211
00:51:07.620 --> 00:51:10.220
Because I just thought Angela wouldn't want to get her hands messy.

1212
00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:13.580
Like, she'd be like, oh, I'm very annoyed at you.

1213
00:51:13.700 --> 00:51:15.720
In fact, I'm incredibly pissed off at you.

1214
00:51:16.140 --> 00:51:18.500
But I'm not going to get this crap all over my hands.

1215
00:51:18.720 --> 00:51:21.520
I guess that tells you how incredibly pissed she was.

1216
00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:23.500
She was willing to dirty her hands.

1217
00:51:23.580 --> 00:51:23.740
Yeah.

1218
00:51:23.740 --> 00:51:24.700
She's super pissed.

1219
00:51:24.700 --> 00:51:26.940
Well, now we're in the stairwell and Oscar.

1220
00:51:26.940 --> 00:51:28.300
The secret stairwell.

1221
00:51:28.360 --> 00:51:29.320
The secret stairwell.

1222
00:51:29.480 --> 00:51:30.200
The secret stairwell.

1223
00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:32.720
Where Jim could have taken any of his business calls.

1224
00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:34.060
Jim's not going to go there.

1225
00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:35.480
But Oscar is.

1226
00:51:35.700 --> 00:51:36.040
Yeah.

1227
00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:37.020
And he's on the phone.

1228
00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:39.760
He's assuring the senator that they are A-OK.

1229
00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:40.380
Yeah.

1230
00:51:40.560 --> 00:51:41.620
Angela doesn't know.

1231
00:51:43.080 --> 00:51:47.420
Dwight, of course, is lurking and has overheard the whole thing.

1232
00:51:47.720 --> 00:51:48.860
He now knows.

1233
00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:50.660
Dun, dun, dun.

1234
00:51:50.860 --> 00:51:51.400
Dun, dun, dun.

1235
00:51:52.380 --> 00:51:54.200
Well, lady, I think we should take a break.

1236
00:51:54.320 --> 00:51:57.860
And when we come back, I have some tower stats for you.

1237
00:51:57.860 --> 00:51:59.820
Oh, I love tower stats.

1238
00:52:00.280 --> 00:52:02.820
I don't even know what a tower stat is.

1239
00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:04.600
But I'm so excited.

1240
00:52:05.600 --> 00:52:06.080
Right.

1241
00:52:17.090 --> 00:52:17.910
We are back.

1242
00:52:18.110 --> 00:52:24.510
And Pam is going to walk into the annex and see everybody building this giant tower.

1243
00:52:24.970 --> 00:52:26.650
And there's all this camaraderie.

1244
00:52:26.770 --> 00:52:30.970
And you can tell she's kind of like, I think she's maybe looking for a reason to not have

1245
00:52:30.970 --> 00:52:32.190
to work on her painting right now.

1246
00:52:32.250 --> 00:52:32.990
I think she's procrastinating.

1247
00:52:33.710 --> 00:52:34.090
She is.

1248
00:52:34.090 --> 00:52:39.730
But she's also very interested in this group activity that looks like a lot of fun.

1249
00:52:39.990 --> 00:52:42.130
The tower is getting taller and taller.

1250
00:52:42.370 --> 00:52:45.170
And Kevin wants to put two cards at the tippity top.

1251
00:52:45.290 --> 00:52:46.530
And everyone's like, Kevin, be careful.

1252
00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:47.070
Be careful.

1253
00:52:47.330 --> 00:52:48.650
And you can guess what happens.

1254
00:52:49.490 --> 00:52:50.930
He knocks the whole thing down.

1255
00:52:51.150 --> 00:52:52.670
But they vow to start over.

1256
00:52:53.130 --> 00:52:54.350
They're going to go again.

1257
00:52:54.690 --> 00:52:54.910
Yeah.

1258
00:52:55.370 --> 00:52:57.710
Well, we got a lot of fan questions about this tower.

1259
00:52:57.910 --> 00:52:58.650
Allison M.

1260
00:52:58.810 --> 00:53:00.730
from Shelby, North Carolina said,

1261
00:53:08.930 --> 00:53:10.270
And Jessica N.

1262
00:53:10.410 --> 00:53:11.970
from Rochester, New York said,

1263
00:53:12.470 --> 00:53:17.570
Do you know if there were any structures that were possibly glued together for some of the scenes?

1264
00:53:17.910 --> 00:53:22.730
Or were they all legit card structures that just didn't make it through some of the takes?

1265
00:53:22.910 --> 00:53:24.330
They had to have been glued together.

1266
00:53:24.670 --> 00:53:25.350
They were.

1267
00:53:25.570 --> 00:53:25.730
Yeah.

1268
00:53:25.730 --> 00:53:28.570
This was such an elaborate prop, actually.

1269
00:53:29.010 --> 00:53:30.770
Kudos again to Phil Shea.

1270
00:53:31.370 --> 00:53:37.610
Steve Burgess said that we had about six different versions of the tower in various stages.

1271
00:53:38.070 --> 00:53:43.890
And then when I was talking with Brent, he reminded me that they built it in layers.

1272
00:53:44.590 --> 00:53:47.930
So there would be like the bottom layer that was all glued together.

1273
00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:51.030
And then we could add like another layer that was all glued together.

1274
00:53:51.030 --> 00:53:56.830
I do remember that, that they would carry them in on almost like this, I don't know, like a tray,

1275
00:53:57.030 --> 00:54:00.430
like a big flat piece of plywood they would carry in a layer at a time.

1276
00:54:00.690 --> 00:54:01.110
Yes.

1277
00:54:01.350 --> 00:54:01.450
Yeah.

1278
00:54:01.570 --> 00:54:05.550
And Steve Burgess actually sent me a bunch of pictures of us posing around the towers.

1279
00:54:05.550 --> 00:54:07.630
So you can put those in stories, Angela.

1280
00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:14.270
But Brent said there was one exception, you know, the scene when Kevin has to knock the tower down.

1281
00:54:14.530 --> 00:54:14.850
Mm-hmm.

1282
00:54:14.850 --> 00:54:20.650
That was a real tower of cards, and it took a very long time to build.

1283
00:54:20.910 --> 00:54:23.090
We filmed that in two parts.

1284
00:54:23.370 --> 00:54:29.750
Brent said we did six takes of the scene right up until the moment when Kevin knocks the tower down.

1285
00:54:30.270 --> 00:54:33.590
Then we did one take of him knocking the tower down.

1286
00:54:33.770 --> 00:54:40.470
And then we did six takes of the second half of the scene when the cards were all splayed out.

1287
00:54:40.570 --> 00:54:40.750
Mm-hmm.

1288
00:54:40.750 --> 00:54:48.430
So he only had to knock the tower down once, but this was really important because you didn't know where the cards were going to go when they fell.

1289
00:54:48.570 --> 00:54:53.750
So it was kind of like an impossible continuity thing to try to recreate the cards falling.

1290
00:54:53.990 --> 00:55:00.010
So that's why once they fell, we just left them where they were, and we did the end of the scene six times.

1291
00:55:00.510 --> 00:55:07.010
Shout out to Brian Baumgartner because this is yet another time where he has to get it basically in one take.

1292
00:55:07.170 --> 00:55:07.350
Mm-hmm.

1293
00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:09.190
He had to spill that whole pot of chili.

1294
00:55:09.470 --> 00:55:09.670
Yep.

1295
00:55:09.670 --> 00:55:11.370
And then one little area.

1296
00:55:11.630 --> 00:55:11.810
Yep.

1297
00:55:11.950 --> 00:55:15.850
And then now he has to knock down this tower of cards and not mess it up.

1298
00:55:16.010 --> 00:55:16.210
Yeah.

1299
00:55:16.450 --> 00:55:20.610
You know, like what if he hit the middle by accident or, you know.

1300
00:55:20.830 --> 00:55:21.230
Yes.

1301
00:55:21.310 --> 00:55:28.730
If it hadn't fallen correctly, we would have had to wait for them to build the whole thing again in order to get the fall right.

1302
00:55:28.930 --> 00:55:29.110
Yeah.

1303
00:55:29.370 --> 00:55:32.050
And then, of course, the subsequent takes afterwards.

1304
00:55:32.370 --> 00:55:33.090
Way to go, Brian.

1305
00:55:33.270 --> 00:55:34.090
Way to go, Brian.

1306
00:55:34.090 --> 00:55:35.990
I would like to share.

1307
00:55:35.990 --> 00:55:39.310
I would like to hear that someone is really noticing how sweet Pete is.

1308
00:55:39.850 --> 00:55:40.230
Mm-hmm.

1309
00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:40.610
Yeah.

1310
00:55:41.090 --> 00:55:45.110
When Kevin knocks it all down and everyone's upset at him, Pete says, you know what?

1311
00:55:45.190 --> 00:55:46.310
He made a mistake.

1312
00:55:46.450 --> 00:55:48.610
And isn't that what that whole tower is about anyway?

1313
00:55:49.010 --> 00:55:52.770
And he gives Kevin a fist bump and he says, let's get back to work.

1314
00:55:53.350 --> 00:55:54.650
Aaron is all smiles.

1315
00:55:54.650 --> 00:55:59.170
And there was a deleted talking head where Aaron talks about Pete.

1316
00:55:59.230 --> 00:56:00.450
And I want you to hear it.

1317
00:56:01.030 --> 00:56:03.070
Pete really sticks up for the underdog.

1318
00:56:03.490 --> 00:56:06.650
And in my book, that's the best dog to stick up for.

1319
00:56:06.850 --> 00:56:11.030
The little dog that's underneath all the other dogs, way at the bottom.

1320
00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:12.710
So, yeah.

1321
00:56:13.130 --> 00:56:14.090
Good guy, this Pete.

1322
00:56:14.290 --> 00:56:15.110
I like this guy.

1323
00:56:15.830 --> 00:56:16.630
Oh, my gosh.

1324
00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:18.590
I love the definition of underdog.

1325
00:56:18.710 --> 00:56:19.210
I know.

1326
00:56:19.620 --> 00:56:24.350
I love the idea that there's a pile of dogs and there's a little tiny one underneath all

1327
00:56:24.350 --> 00:56:24.870
of them.

1328
00:56:25.190 --> 00:56:26.090
Got to get him out of there.

1329
00:56:26.410 --> 00:56:32.190
You know, another thing that Pete says is, come on, who in here hasn't gotten a complaint

1330
00:56:32.190 --> 00:56:33.250
or made a mistake?

1331
00:56:34.250 --> 00:56:36.270
And Pam raises her hand.

1332
00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:38.770
She's never had a complaint against her.

1333
00:56:38.950 --> 00:56:39.930
Not ever?

1334
00:56:39.930 --> 00:56:45.290
With all the pranks she's done with Jim over the years, Dwight hasn't filed a complaint?

1335
00:56:45.290 --> 00:56:47.450
Well, I think it's customer complaint, right?

1336
00:56:47.450 --> 00:56:48.570
Oh, yes, yes, yes.

1337
00:56:48.570 --> 00:56:48.870
Yes.

1338
00:56:48.870 --> 00:56:49.910
Okay, okay, yes.

1339
00:56:49.910 --> 00:56:52.490
I'm sure there's been files of Dwight complaints.

1340
00:56:52.630 --> 00:56:53.510
Oh, yeah, you're right.

1341
00:56:54.010 --> 00:56:58.890
So now the tower has been rebuilt and it is almost up to the ceiling.

1342
00:56:59.210 --> 00:56:59.390
Yeah.

1343
00:56:59.610 --> 00:57:00.850
And they've run out of cards.

1344
00:57:01.090 --> 00:57:02.730
There's no more complaints.

1345
00:57:03.130 --> 00:57:06.370
They just need one more complaint to make it to the ceiling.

1346
00:57:06.850 --> 00:57:09.550
So Pam is going to volunteer to get a complaint.

1347
00:57:10.110 --> 00:57:11.430
Meredith doesn't buy it.

1348
00:57:11.610 --> 00:57:12.690
She says, you know what, Pam?

1349
00:57:12.990 --> 00:57:14.590
She wouldn't fart on a butterfly.

1350
00:57:14.590 --> 00:57:20.650
And then, Jenna, the way you as Pam said the line, I can't even relate to that impulse.

1351
00:57:21.150 --> 00:57:23.410
It was so funny the way you said it.

1352
00:57:23.770 --> 00:57:29.350
Well, one thing I remember from this particular scene was that the first time we had what was

1353
00:57:29.350 --> 00:57:32.150
going to be the tallest tower, Greg said it wasn't tall enough.

1354
00:57:32.890 --> 00:57:33.110
What?

1355
00:57:33.110 --> 00:57:33.670
Yeah.

1356
00:57:33.890 --> 00:57:37.970
He said, I want the top card to touch the ceiling.

1357
00:57:38.410 --> 00:57:40.610
He's like, that's when they know they've done it.

1358
00:57:40.770 --> 00:57:42.590
I have to say, I agree with that.

1359
00:57:42.810 --> 00:57:46.250
That feels like, okay, we've literally reached the ceiling.

1360
00:57:46.450 --> 00:57:46.710
Yes.

1361
00:57:47.170 --> 00:57:52.290
Well, Pam is definitely part of the camaraderie now because everyone is chanting, Pam, Pam, Pam, Pam.

1362
00:57:52.650 --> 00:57:55.690
Except maybe someone in the warehouse would be chanting, Pat, Pat, Pat.

1363
00:57:55.690 --> 00:57:55.930
That's true.

1364
00:57:56.870 --> 00:57:57.770
Very true.

1365
00:57:58.450 --> 00:58:04.390
Well, back at Jim's lunch, Phyllis is, what would you call it?

1366
00:58:04.510 --> 00:58:06.930
She's looking at her hands.

1367
00:58:07.150 --> 00:58:09.630
She's seeing the passage of time.

1368
00:58:09.850 --> 00:58:12.510
I thought Phyllis was so brilliant in this scene.

1369
00:58:12.850 --> 00:58:14.210
She says, you know what?

1370
00:58:14.210 --> 00:58:19.010
I don't know where the years went because sometimes when I look at my hands, I don't even recognize

1371
00:58:19.010 --> 00:58:19.430
them.

1372
00:58:20.030 --> 00:58:22.110
And she goes, whose hands are these?

1373
00:58:22.110 --> 00:58:27.010
And then she kind of folds them into her body and she's like, they're not my hands.

1374
00:58:27.170 --> 00:58:27.870
I don't know.

1375
00:58:28.370 --> 00:58:31.650
And Jim's like, oh, gosh, I'm not getting anywhere.

1376
00:58:31.830 --> 00:58:33.010
Phyllis is so drunk.

1377
00:58:33.330 --> 00:58:35.350
You know what this scene made me think of?

1378
00:58:35.550 --> 00:58:39.210
I remember, I think I must have been in like my mid-40s.

1379
00:58:39.630 --> 00:58:47.270
One morning, I looked down at my hands and I got like immediately emotional because my hands

1380
00:58:47.270 --> 00:58:49.330
looked just like my mother's hands.

1381
00:58:49.470 --> 00:58:49.930
Oh, yeah.

1382
00:58:49.930 --> 00:58:56.530
And I thought one day she's not going to be here anymore, but she's always going to be

1383
00:58:56.530 --> 00:58:57.410
in my hands.

1384
00:58:57.650 --> 00:58:57.850
Yeah.

1385
00:58:57.990 --> 00:58:58.990
I'm going to see her.

1386
00:58:59.150 --> 00:59:02.130
And I do see her when I look at my hands, even now.

1387
00:59:02.470 --> 00:59:03.870
It's so special to me.

1388
00:59:04.210 --> 00:59:04.430
Yeah.

1389
00:59:04.790 --> 00:59:05.190
Yeah.

1390
00:59:05.350 --> 00:59:10.450
I started looking more and more like my mother as I get older, even in my face and everything

1391
00:59:10.450 --> 00:59:11.030
like that.

1392
00:59:11.190 --> 00:59:13.590
Growing up, I thought I didn't really look like either of my parents.

1393
00:59:13.590 --> 00:59:18.770
So when Phyllis was giving this big speech about whose hands are these, I like remember

1394
00:59:18.770 --> 00:59:23.350
the day I felt like I went from having my hands to, oh, my gosh, I have my mother's

1395
00:59:23.350 --> 00:59:23.750
hands.

1396
00:59:24.210 --> 00:59:24.310
Yeah.

1397
00:59:24.610 --> 00:59:24.830
Yeah.

1398
00:59:25.250 --> 00:59:26.350
No, I get that too.

1399
00:59:26.670 --> 00:59:30.510
My dad's index fingers turned in and so do mine.

1400
00:59:31.070 --> 00:59:31.930
Yours do.

1401
00:59:32.350 --> 00:59:32.630
Yeah.

1402
00:59:32.630 --> 00:59:34.530
I never noticed that about you.

1403
00:59:34.710 --> 00:59:35.010
Yeah.

1404
00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:35.970
And he's—

1405
00:59:35.970 --> 00:59:36.610
I see it.

1406
00:59:37.110 --> 00:59:42.390
He's no longer here, but I think about that when I look at my hands.

1407
00:59:43.630 --> 00:59:44.110
Sorry.

1408
00:59:44.310 --> 00:59:44.890
That was okay.

1409
00:59:46.590 --> 00:59:47.490
This is like—

1410
00:59:47.490 --> 00:59:48.130
Sorry, everyone.

1411
00:59:48.130 --> 00:59:50.050
We went real deep there for a second.

1412
00:59:50.290 --> 00:59:50.710
We did.

1413
00:59:51.010 --> 00:59:51.310
Sorry.

1414
00:59:51.930 --> 00:59:52.270
Ugh.

1415
00:59:52.870 --> 00:59:55.710
These are more of the things I think you think about as you age.

1416
00:59:55.870 --> 00:59:56.390
You do.

1417
00:59:56.610 --> 00:59:57.130
You do.

1418
00:59:57.250 --> 00:59:58.050
Passage of time.

1419
00:59:58.390 --> 00:59:58.830
Passage of time.

1420
00:59:59.030 --> 00:59:59.750
Like Phyllis is talking about.

1421
00:59:59.750 --> 01:00:01.570
Phyllis is having a moment like that.

1422
01:00:01.570 --> 01:00:02.330
She is.

1423
01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:07.090
Which is not helping Jim as he's trying to just get her to cover for him when he goes

1424
01:00:07.090 --> 01:00:07.630
out of town.

1425
01:00:07.810 --> 01:00:10.290
No, because how do you segue from that, right?

1426
01:00:10.430 --> 01:00:15.390
Could you imagine us having the conversation we just had about our hands and then Cassie

1427
01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:20.890
had to walk in here and be like, so listen, are y'all going to be able to cover for—

1428
01:00:21.290 --> 01:00:22.530
Can you wrap it up?

1429
01:00:22.630 --> 01:00:23.590
Just bring up business.

1430
01:00:23.850 --> 01:00:24.030
Yeah.

1431
01:00:24.390 --> 01:00:24.690
Yes.

1432
01:00:24.770 --> 01:00:26.470
Very difficult position he is in.

1433
01:00:27.030 --> 01:00:30.650
Well, if you thought that was the worst it was going to be for Jim, it's not because

1434
01:00:30.650 --> 01:00:38.190
in a later scene, Phyllis starts poking a fellow patron with a knife because she wants him

1435
01:00:38.190 --> 01:00:45.330
to help her pry that ginormous decorative bottle that it looks like it's been super glued down

1436
01:00:45.330 --> 01:00:46.530
to the banister.

1437
01:00:46.790 --> 01:00:47.310
Yeah.

1438
01:00:47.410 --> 01:00:48.570
But she wants to drink it.

1439
01:00:48.710 --> 01:00:53.230
That reminded me of those bottles of wine they would put on the tables at the SAG Awards.

1440
01:00:53.310 --> 01:00:53.510
Right.

1441
01:00:53.630 --> 01:00:54.870
The one that Matt Damon took.

1442
01:00:55.570 --> 01:01:01.210
We got a fan question from Becca G in Morganfield, Kentucky, who said,

1443
01:01:01.350 --> 01:01:05.370
the scene that makes me laugh the hardest and it only gets funnier every time is when

1444
01:01:05.370 --> 01:01:07.310
Jim, Stanley, and Phyllis go to lunch.

1445
01:01:07.550 --> 01:01:12.370
Every time Phyllis goes for that decorative wine, I cry from laughing so hard.

1446
01:01:12.710 --> 01:01:15.150
Those three were phenomenal in that scene.

1447
01:01:15.330 --> 01:01:15.790
So good.

1448
01:01:15.790 --> 01:01:24.270
So Brent told us that this shoot at the restaurant went so smoothly and so quickly that they got

1449
01:01:24.270 --> 01:01:29.790
ahead of schedule and they had time to write an extra scene on the spot and shoot it.

1450
01:01:30.210 --> 01:01:36.210
And it was a scene where Stanley drags Jim into the kitchen and makes him pay the cook to

1451
01:01:36.210 --> 01:01:38.190
create a custom dessert for the table.

1452
01:01:38.390 --> 01:01:39.810
It's in deleted scenes.

1453
01:01:40.050 --> 01:01:41.770
Part of it is in deleted scenes.

1454
01:01:41.770 --> 01:01:47.370
He just starts taking like plates of dessert off like, you know, he's in the kitchen in

1455
01:01:47.370 --> 01:01:50.210
the back where they're plating things and Stanley just starts grabbing them.

1456
01:01:50.570 --> 01:01:55.250
Brent said the scene was so funny, but unfortunately there was no room for it in the final cut.

1457
01:01:55.590 --> 01:02:00.110
But I love that they were on set and they were like, we need, we got to write something

1458
01:02:00.110 --> 01:02:01.110
right now for these three.

1459
01:02:01.110 --> 01:02:01.810
What else can they do?

1460
01:02:01.810 --> 01:02:02.510
This is going so well.

1461
01:02:03.050 --> 01:02:06.910
Back at Dundra Mifflin, Dwight has called Angela out to the parking lot and he tells her,

1462
01:02:07.070 --> 01:02:08.330
I know who your target is.

1463
01:02:08.370 --> 01:02:09.030
It's Oscar.

1464
01:02:09.490 --> 01:02:09.850
Yeah.

1465
01:02:09.850 --> 01:02:15.090
Well, she pretends like she doesn't know what Dwight is talking about, but he says your

1466
01:02:15.090 --> 01:02:18.350
nostrils are flaring and they tell another story.

1467
01:02:18.490 --> 01:02:20.930
Yeah, you're lying because of your nostrils.

1468
01:02:21.230 --> 01:02:23.610
He says your naughty nostrils.

1469
01:02:24.350 --> 01:02:27.150
Well, of course I had to look up facial signs of lying.

1470
01:02:27.710 --> 01:02:29.690
Is flaring nostrils one of them?

1471
01:02:29.870 --> 01:02:30.330
No.

1472
01:02:30.630 --> 01:02:30.950
Oh.

1473
01:02:31.350 --> 01:02:33.250
No, Dwight is not right.

1474
01:02:33.470 --> 01:02:36.890
Unless somewhere else on the internet there's something to prove me wrong.

1475
01:02:36.890 --> 01:02:42.190
No, I think Dwight is right, but it's just a particular tell of Angela's, not in general.

1476
01:02:42.430 --> 01:02:50.110
The most common tells of facial lying are pursed lips, blushing, blinking, darting eyes, avoiding

1477
01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:55.930
eye contact, face touching, covering your mouth, head shaking, and sweating.

1478
01:02:55.930 --> 01:02:59.630
I knew that touching your face is a sign of lying.

1479
01:03:00.090 --> 01:03:00.530
How?

1480
01:03:00.830 --> 01:03:02.850
I remember that from my poker days.

1481
01:03:03.070 --> 01:03:03.490
Oh.

1482
01:03:03.850 --> 01:03:08.670
Many of the things you mentioned are also poker tells for when someone is bluffing.

1483
01:03:08.970 --> 01:03:09.490
Oh.

1484
01:03:09.710 --> 01:03:13.690
Like flushing of the cheeks, but also like touching of the face.

1485
01:03:14.590 --> 01:03:15.110
Interesting.

1486
01:03:15.850 --> 01:03:18.870
Well, flared nostrils isn't on the liSt.

1487
01:03:19.570 --> 01:03:24.450
I really like Dwight in this scene because he reminds Angela that the first person to

1488
01:03:24.450 --> 01:03:26.530
break her marriage vows is her.

1489
01:03:27.010 --> 01:03:28.530
It was the two of them.

1490
01:03:28.730 --> 01:03:29.370
That's right.

1491
01:03:29.470 --> 01:03:33.110
And she goes, well, you might be right, but it's too late.

1492
01:03:33.290 --> 01:03:33.810
He's here.

1493
01:03:34.450 --> 01:03:35.890
The plan is in motion.

1494
01:03:36.270 --> 01:03:39.150
Yeah, they look over, there's Trevor's van, and it's empty.

1495
01:03:39.470 --> 01:03:40.830
Trevor is on the move.

1496
01:03:41.010 --> 01:03:42.330
Dwight is now panicked.

1497
01:03:42.330 --> 01:03:46.050
He's got to find Oscar, so he rushes into the office.

1498
01:03:46.210 --> 01:03:46.910
He grabs him.

1499
01:03:47.010 --> 01:03:49.490
He's making up an excuse to get him down to the warehouse.

1500
01:03:50.070 --> 01:03:55.670
Meanwhile, just as they get out of there, Trevor enters with a sub-sandwich, like a

1501
01:03:55.670 --> 01:03:59.090
big sandwich, and he has a metal pipe in it.

1502
01:03:59.210 --> 01:04:02.130
You can clearly see the metal pipe.

1503
01:04:02.270 --> 01:04:02.970
It's so funny.

1504
01:04:03.130 --> 01:04:08.850
He announces that he has a sandwich for Oscar Martinez, and Kevin says, I am Oscar Martinez.

1505
01:04:09.270 --> 01:04:11.390
And then Angela comes in and goes, no, no, no.

1506
01:04:11.390 --> 01:04:15.050
And tells Kevin there are donuts, so Kevin will get out of the way.

1507
01:04:15.250 --> 01:04:21.590
Well, we asked Chris if he ever got recognized for being on the office, and he has a very

1508
01:04:21.590 --> 01:04:22.490
funny story.

1509
01:04:22.850 --> 01:04:26.950
And he referenced this sandwich delivery moment specifically.

1510
01:04:28.330 --> 01:04:34.450
As far as if I get recognized as Trevor from the office, holy s***, it's non-stop.

1511
01:04:34.870 --> 01:04:42.470
To the point where I've said, and again, to Angela, Jenna, I say it to you as well.

1512
01:04:42.470 --> 01:04:49.930
I've kept in touch with Rain to a degree, and I'm like, man, I don't know how you go out

1513
01:04:49.930 --> 01:04:54.570
for dinner or go to the airport, because I was on two episodes of this damn show, and

1514
01:04:54.570 --> 01:04:56.550
I got stopped all the time.

1515
01:04:57.630 --> 01:04:59.730
And it's been so many years.

1516
01:04:59.890 --> 01:05:02.550
It's been, I think, over a decade at this point.

1517
01:05:03.070 --> 01:05:07.850
And to be clear, I had an HBO special, stand-up special.

1518
01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:11.550
I had a show called The Chris Gethard Show.

1519
01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:14.430
I've been on other things, Parks and Rec.

1520
01:05:14.910 --> 01:05:18.210
I've been on Conan a bunch of times as myself, where they're saying my name.

1521
01:05:18.350 --> 01:05:19.390
I've been on Inside A.B.

1522
01:05:19.510 --> 01:05:19.790
Schumer.

1523
01:05:19.910 --> 01:05:23.610
Like, I don't want to list a million credits, but I've had a good career as a character actor.

1524
01:05:24.550 --> 01:05:28.250
Nothing gets recognized more than just, hey, weren't you the guy with the pipe?

1525
01:05:28.570 --> 01:05:30.670
Weren't you the guy, the sandwich delivery guy?

1526
01:05:30.670 --> 01:05:32.370
I get that all the time still.

1527
01:05:32.850 --> 01:05:38.930
I would say, if I get recognized, there's an 85% chance it's for the office.

1528
01:05:39.370 --> 01:05:45.630
Like, no joke, just last weekend, did stand-up in San Francisco and Portland, and my bag got

1529
01:05:45.630 --> 01:05:46.550
pulled by TSA.

1530
01:05:47.310 --> 01:05:49.590
And this is not an exaggeration at all.

1531
01:05:50.050 --> 01:05:55.830
The TSA agent, he didn't say anything to me at first, but he patted down my chest, and

1532
01:05:55.830 --> 01:05:58.570
then my actual ass and testicles.

1533
01:05:58.570 --> 01:06:03.010
He touched my testicles with his hands, popped up, and went, you're good to go.

1534
01:06:03.130 --> 01:06:04.390
I loved you on the office.

1535
01:06:05.230 --> 01:06:06.930
That is not an exaggeration.

1536
01:06:07.090 --> 01:06:13.170
Like, a TSA agent fondled my testicles and then told me he really liked me as Trevor on

1537
01:06:13.170 --> 01:06:13.610
the office.

1538
01:06:13.610 --> 01:06:20.870
So, it is hugely flattering to be part of something so meaningful that such a small part struck

1539
01:06:20.870 --> 01:06:21.830
a chord with people.

1540
01:06:22.530 --> 01:06:23.630
It's awesome.

1541
01:06:23.890 --> 01:06:24.830
It's an honor.

1542
01:06:25.130 --> 01:06:30.750
I also sit here and I go, for those of you who were series regulars every episode for 10

1543
01:06:30.750 --> 01:06:37.850
years, God bless you all, because I've had my nuts touched by a TSA agent who immediately

1544
01:06:37.850 --> 01:06:38.990
then brought up the office.

1545
01:06:38.990 --> 01:06:41.710
So, I can't imagine what you all go through on a regular basis.

1546
01:06:43.730 --> 01:06:47.210
Chris, I absolutely love that story.

1547
01:06:47.510 --> 01:06:51.950
I mean, we both have so many versions of that story.

1548
01:06:52.430 --> 01:06:54.330
It does happen all the time.

1549
01:06:54.890 --> 01:06:57.210
But you just can't help but laugh.

1550
01:06:57.270 --> 01:06:58.490
I mean, my goodness.

1551
01:06:58.790 --> 01:07:00.930
It's also the timing of when it happens.

1552
01:07:01.130 --> 01:07:03.030
Like, this guy just frisked you.

1553
01:07:03.070 --> 01:07:03.430
Yes, exactly.

1554
01:07:03.430 --> 01:07:04.530
And then was like, by the way.

1555
01:07:05.110 --> 01:07:08.390
Yes, and also because you've been frisked, right?

1556
01:07:08.550 --> 01:07:08.690
Yeah.

1557
01:07:08.690 --> 01:07:11.730
And you think you have anonymity while the frisking is happening.

1558
01:07:11.910 --> 01:07:12.130
Yes.

1559
01:07:12.250 --> 01:07:15.650
And then as you're leaving the frisk, you realize, oh.

1560
01:07:16.090 --> 01:07:16.510
Yes.

1561
01:07:16.730 --> 01:07:17.130
Okay.

1562
01:07:17.590 --> 01:07:19.070
That's just one example.

1563
01:07:19.090 --> 01:07:19.770
Just one day.

1564
01:07:19.970 --> 01:07:20.590
Of hundreds.

1565
01:07:20.590 --> 01:07:24.290
There are truly so many examples, just like Chris's.

1566
01:07:24.650 --> 01:07:25.690
Yes, there are.

1567
01:07:25.790 --> 01:07:26.470
We have so many.

1568
01:07:26.670 --> 01:07:30.190
But, you know, obviously, we're so appreciative that people love the show.

1569
01:07:30.330 --> 01:07:32.050
But sometimes the timing.

1570
01:07:32.450 --> 01:07:34.130
Sometimes the timing could be better.

1571
01:07:34.530 --> 01:07:37.490
Maybe mention it before the frisk.

1572
01:07:37.630 --> 01:07:39.490
I think you want it after the frisk.

1573
01:07:39.810 --> 01:07:42.990
I do think if you're going to say something, do it after a frisk.

1574
01:07:43.130 --> 01:07:43.370
After the.

1575
01:07:43.550 --> 01:07:44.430
I think.

1576
01:07:44.630 --> 01:07:44.890
Yeah.

1577
01:07:45.150 --> 01:07:45.430
Okay.

1578
01:07:45.690 --> 01:07:45.950
Right?

1579
01:07:46.350 --> 01:07:47.330
Yeah, maybe so.

1580
01:07:47.470 --> 01:07:48.250
I think so.

1581
01:07:48.970 --> 01:07:51.110
Well, now Dwight and Oscar are in the warehouse.

1582
01:07:51.670 --> 01:07:56.750
And Dwight tells him what was going on, that they were going to break his kneecaps.

1583
01:07:57.290 --> 01:08:00.650
And he says, of all the gorgeous men in the Scranton-Wilkes-Barre area,

1584
01:08:00.650 --> 01:08:03.150
why did you have to choose the father of Angela's child?

1585
01:08:03.990 --> 01:08:04.870
Come on.

1586
01:08:05.310 --> 01:08:07.570
Dwight is still trying to just get Oscar out of there.

1587
01:08:07.690 --> 01:08:10.190
He rushes them out of the warehouse into the alleyway.

1588
01:08:10.390 --> 01:08:11.230
And guess what?

1589
01:08:11.390 --> 01:08:12.450
Trevor is right there.

1590
01:08:12.530 --> 01:08:16.010
And he is determined for once to follow through with something.

1591
01:08:16.450 --> 01:08:21.610
So the three of them end up kind of scuffling, struggling over this pipe.

1592
01:08:21.890 --> 01:08:23.750
Dwight and Oscar are trying to get it away from Trevor.

1593
01:08:23.930 --> 01:08:26.430
Trevor's trying to complete the job for which he was hired.

1594
01:08:27.210 --> 01:08:28.090
Oscar prevails.

1595
01:08:28.090 --> 01:08:29.110
He gets the pipe.

1596
01:08:29.490 --> 01:08:33.950
I asked Steve Burgess if we had a fight coordinator or a stunt coordinator for this fight.

1597
01:08:33.990 --> 01:08:34.950
And he said no.

1598
01:08:35.270 --> 01:08:35.530
No.

1599
01:08:35.930 --> 01:08:39.150
They just let the three of them sort it out.

1600
01:08:39.310 --> 01:08:39.590
Yeah.

1601
01:08:40.210 --> 01:08:42.050
Well, I asked Chris about this scene.

1602
01:08:42.070 --> 01:08:43.690
And here's what he had to say.

1603
01:08:43.690 --> 01:08:48.930
As far as the fight scene, I remember the day moving pretty quickly.

1604
01:08:49.210 --> 01:08:54.510
I remember being pretty intimidated, even though obviously everyone on set was so nice.

1605
01:08:54.830 --> 01:08:56.930
But I felt like things could get wild.

1606
01:08:57.170 --> 01:08:58.390
But it all went well.

1607
01:08:58.390 --> 01:09:03.310
I remember being slightly different each time because we were kind of really wrestling around.

1608
01:09:03.790 --> 01:09:06.310
I think I like that.

1609
01:09:06.550 --> 01:09:11.110
I like the idea of if we're going to do something physical and we're not supposed to be trained fighters,

1610
01:09:11.170 --> 01:09:14.790
like we might as well go for it and see where our bodies go because that'll be natural.

1611
01:09:14.890 --> 01:09:19.710
I also got the sense that Rain was probably into that.

1612
01:09:20.090 --> 01:09:24.470
Oscar, I think, was probably the one who was the most reserved about, wait, what's exactly going to happen?

1613
01:09:24.470 --> 01:09:33.250
And I think Rain and I have remained, you know, not super tight, but we touch base every couple of years just to see how the other one's doing and cross paths.

1614
01:09:33.450 --> 01:09:41.190
And I do get the sense he and I both probably have a similar sense of, well, if we're doing some scene that's supposed to be some crazy unplanned wrestling match,

1615
01:09:41.330 --> 01:09:44.470
the easiest way to do it is just let's start wrestling.

1616
01:09:44.890 --> 01:09:46.030
So that was pretty fun.

1617
01:09:47.170 --> 01:09:51.030
Chris, I would confirm that that would be Rain's take on that.

1618
01:09:51.190 --> 01:09:51.970
A hundred percent.

1619
01:09:52.210 --> 01:09:52.470
Yes.

1620
01:09:52.470 --> 01:09:59.830
I also asked Chris if any of his improvs made it into the episode because, you know, he was so funny.

1621
01:10:00.050 --> 01:10:02.990
And yes, we got everything scripted and it was so well written.

1622
01:10:03.310 --> 01:10:06.530
But there were these great moments of improvisation from him.

1623
01:10:06.650 --> 01:10:07.810
And here's what he had to say.

1624
01:10:08.710 --> 01:10:12.610
I do remember improvising a line in the fight with Oscar.

1625
01:10:13.350 --> 01:10:15.590
If I remember right, I run away.

1626
01:10:15.590 --> 01:10:22.030
And while I run away, I'm shouting something along the lines of I have masculinity issues.

1627
01:10:22.470 --> 01:10:24.450
And that line was improvised.

1628
01:10:24.650 --> 01:10:27.590
I remember being psyched that they used that.

1629
01:10:28.630 --> 01:10:30.010
Oh, that is so fun.

1630
01:10:30.170 --> 01:10:34.130
Chris also had talked about just how amazing the writing was.

1631
01:10:34.530 --> 01:10:36.690
And he said, like,

1632
01:10:36.930 --> 01:10:47.570
one of the reasons he was so tickled to get an improv line in was because he knew it was going to have to really be good in order to make it because all the scripted stuff was so good.

1633
01:10:47.570 --> 01:10:47.850
Yeah.

1634
01:10:48.390 --> 01:10:51.210
Well, we got a fan question about this scene from Carly P.

1635
01:10:51.430 --> 01:10:52.270
in Cesar, Illinois.

1636
01:10:53.010 --> 01:11:02.650
And Carly said this, when Dwight, Oscar, and Trevor are fighting over the pipe, there are people passing by in the background on the street.

1637
01:11:03.010 --> 01:11:08.450
Were these just actual passerbys or were they staged to walk past at this point?

1638
01:11:08.630 --> 01:11:13.090
Because they do not look concerned about three grown men rolling on the ground fighting over a lead pipe.

1639
01:11:14.210 --> 01:11:16.830
Carly, I did not notice this at firSt.

1640
01:11:16.830 --> 01:11:20.690
I went back and I rewatched the scene and I see this couple walk by.

1641
01:11:21.310 --> 01:11:24.370
I think they're just two people who walked down the street.

1642
01:11:24.670 --> 01:11:26.850
Carly, I went back and looked as well.

1643
01:11:27.090 --> 01:11:29.990
They are 100% just two people walking down the street.

1644
01:11:30.830 --> 01:11:38.390
I think the other thing that's going on here is that we had been filming this show for nine years at this location.

1645
01:11:38.470 --> 01:11:41.350
And there were other businesses right next to us.

1646
01:11:41.470 --> 01:11:44.630
And at this point, they just knew, oh, it's that TV show.

1647
01:11:44.630 --> 01:11:51.670
So I think when they saw three men, like, tussling, they were like, oh, they're doing, they're filming something.

1648
01:11:51.670 --> 01:11:52.330
I don't know.

1649
01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:54.010
That is exactly what I thought as well.

1650
01:11:54.290 --> 01:11:58.370
Well, now it's Pam's turn to try to get a complaint, right?

1651
01:11:58.610 --> 01:11:58.790
Yep.

1652
01:11:59.170 --> 01:12:02.570
Erin and Nellie and Creed are really encouraging her to fail.

1653
01:12:02.570 --> 01:12:05.150
Well, they say you got to think like a scumbag.

1654
01:12:05.590 --> 01:12:07.730
Creed shows her, but then just looks off into space.

1655
01:12:07.870 --> 01:12:08.970
It's like this.

1656
01:12:09.470 --> 01:12:15.610
Pam calls a client and does your mama's so fat joke and hangs up on them.

1657
01:12:16.690 --> 01:12:20.030
Erin's phone immediately rings and she takes the message.

1658
01:12:20.150 --> 01:12:20.650
And guess what?

1659
01:12:20.970 --> 01:12:22.190
They lost a client.

1660
01:12:22.330 --> 01:12:22.710
Woo-hoo!

1661
01:12:22.710 --> 01:12:23.730
Everyone's like, woo-hoo!

1662
01:12:23.910 --> 01:12:25.110
But then they're like, oh, wait, crap.

1663
01:12:25.250 --> 01:12:26.150
We lost a client.

1664
01:12:26.150 --> 01:12:27.830
That was, we just wanted a complaint.

1665
01:12:28.230 --> 01:12:28.430
A complaint.

1666
01:12:29.250 --> 01:12:31.370
Jenna, you're so good in this scene.

1667
01:12:31.630 --> 01:12:33.590
I thought you just were great.

1668
01:12:33.730 --> 01:12:34.590
And you know what?

1669
01:12:34.730 --> 01:12:35.690
Brent thought so too.

1670
01:12:35.910 --> 01:12:36.870
Here's what he had to say.

1671
01:12:37.570 --> 01:12:45.830
The Tower of Complaints subplot is one of those big group stories that becomes so useful to the writers in the later seasons of The Office.

1672
01:12:45.830 --> 01:12:50.610
When we have 14 characters in the show and we have to give them all something to do.

1673
01:12:50.770 --> 01:12:54.310
So this one takes up eight characters and that's really useful to the writers.

1674
01:12:54.510 --> 01:12:59.610
What was so fun about this episode was how funny Jenna was in it.

1675
01:12:59.750 --> 01:13:03.530
So often Pam's character is the emotional heart of the story.

1676
01:13:03.750 --> 01:13:08.430
And her relationship with Jim is the romantic subplot that is driving so much of the series.

1677
01:13:08.590 --> 01:13:11.630
But it is so delightful when Jenna gets to be funny.

1678
01:13:11.630 --> 01:13:20.990
And the writers took a lot of pleasure in figuring out how to make this proper character funny by forcing her to fail.

1679
01:13:21.250 --> 01:13:29.630
And you'll notice that in the final prank phone call that she makes to lose a client, the laugh doesn't come on the yo mama joke.

1680
01:13:29.810 --> 01:13:37.590
It comes on Pam having to over explain the yo mama joke because she's never had to deliver an insult like this before.

1681
01:13:37.590 --> 01:13:45.730
It's a classic example of the laugh coming, not on the cleverness of the joke itself, but on the performance of the actor.

1682
01:13:45.950 --> 01:13:46.970
And Jenna just nails it.

1683
01:13:47.830 --> 01:13:55.690
Lady, my favorite thing about Brent sending in audio clips is that they always include like high praise for you and I.

1684
01:13:56.630 --> 01:13:57.310
I know, Brent.

1685
01:13:57.310 --> 01:13:59.090
And they make me feel so good about myself.

1686
01:13:59.490 --> 01:14:01.030
Brent, thank you so much.

1687
01:14:01.030 --> 01:14:13.870
But one of the other things I really appreciate about Brent's perspective on episodes is he always takes it back to what moves the story, what makes the joke happen.

1688
01:14:14.350 --> 01:14:19.390
I can just hear what a great teacher he is because he's so right.

1689
01:14:19.830 --> 01:14:22.870
The thing that's funny about it isn't the yo mama joke.

1690
01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:28.670
It's Pam getting so flustered and trying so earnestly to really be off-putting.

1691
01:14:28.670 --> 01:14:29.290
Yes.

1692
01:14:29.690 --> 01:14:31.570
Speaking of, Brent is a teacher.

1693
01:14:31.790 --> 01:14:33.670
He teaches comedy writing classes.

1694
01:14:33.990 --> 01:14:34.270
Yes.

1695
01:14:34.430 --> 01:14:37.490
And you can sign up for his classes at brentforrester.com.

1696
01:14:37.690 --> 01:14:39.130
And you know I'll put it in stories.

1697
01:14:39.330 --> 01:14:40.030
Yes, she will.

1698
01:14:40.450 --> 01:14:50.930
Well, this storyline ends with everybody back in the annex and they put Pam's complaint on the top of the tower and it's almost brushing the ceiling tiles and everyone cheers.

1699
01:14:51.550 --> 01:14:53.530
Yeah, Pam saved the day.

1700
01:14:53.530 --> 01:14:54.310
Yes,

1701
01:14:54.470 --> 01:15:06.770
but we learn a little more information about how her insult was truly terrible because I guess this woman had recently passed away and had struggled with obesity her whole life.

1702
01:15:07.010 --> 01:15:11.490
The client's mother had just recently passed away, which is why it wasn't just a complaint card.

1703
01:15:11.630 --> 01:15:13.650
It was, you don't get our business anymore.

1704
01:15:13.750 --> 01:15:14.030
Yes.

1705
01:15:14.110 --> 01:15:14.530
This is horrible.

1706
01:15:14.530 --> 01:15:17.570
And Pam wants you to know I did not know any of that information.

1707
01:15:17.570 --> 01:15:18.730
I am so very sorry.

1708
01:15:18.730 --> 01:15:19.510
Of course not.

1709
01:15:19.870 --> 01:15:22.490
But everyone's happy.

1710
01:15:22.790 --> 01:15:23.830
They built a tower.

1711
01:15:25.050 --> 01:15:26.430
That's what they did that day.

1712
01:15:26.910 --> 01:15:27.890
So simple.

1713
01:15:28.090 --> 01:15:28.650
So simple.

1714
01:15:28.770 --> 01:15:29.230
And lovely.

1715
01:15:30.290 --> 01:15:39.910
We also got a fan question about this scene from Royelle L in Boise, Idaho, who said, I have been waiting for years to submit this.

1716
01:15:40.070 --> 01:15:45.950
It always makes me so happy when we can, like, answer a question for someone that has been, like, years in making.

1717
01:15:45.950 --> 01:15:46.730
Waiting for the episode.

1718
01:15:46.930 --> 01:15:47.150
Yes.

1719
01:15:48.730 --> 01:15:48.790
Great.

1720
01:15:48.850 --> 01:15:49.550
Here's the question.

1721
01:15:49.830 --> 01:15:56.030
When Creed is congratulating Pam for her first customer complaint, why is he in gray sweatpants?

1722
01:15:56.430 --> 01:16:00.610
I also noticed he has his business shirt tucked into his sweatpants.

1723
01:16:00.850 --> 01:16:02.490
This is such a funny detail.

1724
01:16:02.850 --> 01:16:08.990
Well, Royelle, I noticed this too, and I was curious, so I went to the shooting draft and I found the answer.

1725
01:16:09.350 --> 01:16:13.670
It was actually explained at the very top of the episode, and here's how the script read.

1726
01:16:14.190 --> 01:16:16.070
Jim and Pam walk in for the day.

1727
01:16:16.070 --> 01:16:20.810
Pam is wearing painting clothes, a paint-flecked sweatshirt and jeans.

1728
01:16:21.150 --> 01:16:23.630
Dwight says, well, it finally happened.

1729
01:16:23.830 --> 01:16:24.710
Pam, stop caring.

1730
01:16:25.390 --> 01:16:27.210
Creed says, welcome aboard, Pam.

1731
01:16:27.450 --> 01:16:31.930
And in the stage direction, it says, Creed is wearing sweatpants and Crocs.

1732
01:16:31.930 --> 01:16:34.450
Oh, wow, that's great.

1733
01:16:34.470 --> 01:16:37.150
And then Pam says, these are my painting clothes.

1734
01:16:37.710 --> 01:16:41.390
Isn't it funny how you trim out one line?

1735
01:16:41.650 --> 01:16:41.990
Yeah.

1736
01:16:42.390 --> 01:16:46.190
And now for this whole episode, we have the mystery of why is Creed wearing sweatpants?

1737
01:16:46.410 --> 01:16:50.150
I bet Creed was so happy to be wearing sweatpants and Crocs all week.

1738
01:16:50.150 --> 01:16:53.750
Like, he was probably like, we should make this a weekly thing.

1739
01:16:54.410 --> 01:17:01.610
Back in the alleyway, the guys have stopped tussling, and Angela now has suddenly appeared behind Oscar.

1740
01:17:01.910 --> 01:17:02.190
Yeah.

1741
01:17:02.610 --> 01:17:03.750
And he's going to yell at her.

1742
01:17:03.870 --> 01:17:08.930
He's going to be like, I cannot believe you hired someone to beat me with a pipe.

1743
01:17:08.930 --> 01:17:13.550
And he says, look, what I did was wrong, and I have to live with that every day, but your husband is gay.

1744
01:17:13.730 --> 01:17:15.490
And he was gay when you married him.

1745
01:17:15.550 --> 01:17:16.550
And Angela's like, no.

1746
01:17:17.250 --> 01:17:22.730
And Oscar says, Angela, until you face that, you're going to be confused and angry for all the wrong reasons.

1747
01:17:22.890 --> 01:17:25.390
But if you want to blame me for the whole thing, go ahead.

1748
01:17:25.510 --> 01:17:26.410
I won't stop you.

1749
01:17:26.790 --> 01:17:27.190
Hit me.

1750
01:17:27.350 --> 01:17:28.430
You have my blessing.

1751
01:17:28.750 --> 01:17:29.250
Hit me.

1752
01:17:30.390 --> 01:17:31.570
And Angela's like, okay.

1753
01:17:31.710 --> 01:17:32.750
And she grabs the pipe.

1754
01:17:32.810 --> 01:17:34.470
I know, but he won't let go of it.

1755
01:17:34.990 --> 01:17:37.330
And she says, are you going to let go of it?

1756
01:17:37.330 --> 01:17:39.430
Because part of the blame is definitely on you.

1757
01:17:39.630 --> 01:17:42.810
And then Oscar says, Angela, it's a lead freaking pipe.

1758
01:17:42.990 --> 01:17:44.510
And then Angela just kicks him.

1759
01:17:44.730 --> 01:17:46.110
How did that work, Angela?

1760
01:17:46.350 --> 01:17:48.210
Did you just fake kick him?

1761
01:17:48.290 --> 01:17:49.030
Did he have a pad?

1762
01:17:49.330 --> 01:17:51.530
I kicked him, and he had a pad on his shin.

1763
01:17:51.650 --> 01:17:52.570
Like a shin guard.

1764
01:17:52.670 --> 01:17:54.590
He had a shin guard, but I really kicked him.

1765
01:17:54.750 --> 01:17:56.470
Okay, because it looked like a real kick.

1766
01:17:56.590 --> 01:17:57.090
It was.

1767
01:17:57.230 --> 01:17:57.490
Okay.

1768
01:17:58.270 --> 01:18:03.190
And then Angela very emotionally says, you were supposed to be my friend.

1769
01:18:03.550 --> 01:18:03.870
Yeah.

1770
01:18:04.450 --> 01:18:07.170
And then later, Angela is crying.

1771
01:18:07.330 --> 01:18:09.970
On a bench outside, and Dwight joins her.

1772
01:18:10.270 --> 01:18:12.830
And she says, I just feel so stupid.

1773
01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:15.710
I sit next to him every day.

1774
01:18:16.950 --> 01:18:21.110
So lady, this is a line that's quoted to me a lot.

1775
01:18:21.350 --> 01:18:21.810
Okay.

1776
01:18:21.970 --> 01:18:22.890
This is one of those lines.

1777
01:18:23.270 --> 01:18:24.630
And Rain gets it too.

1778
01:18:24.750 --> 01:18:25.570
You're not stupid.

1779
01:18:25.730 --> 01:18:26.550
Jazz is stupid.

1780
01:18:26.830 --> 01:18:28.890
And then I say, jazz is stupid.

1781
01:18:29.030 --> 01:18:30.530
I mean, just play the right notes.

1782
01:18:30.530 --> 01:18:32.230
It's such a funny thing to say.

1783
01:18:32.490 --> 01:18:34.090
And then Dwight says, I know.

1784
01:18:34.270 --> 01:18:35.570
You're going to be okay, monkey.

1785
01:18:35.570 --> 01:18:39.630
And then the last couplet of dialogue was completely improvised.

1786
01:18:39.950 --> 01:18:40.890
About Trevor?

1787
01:18:41.450 --> 01:18:44.110
I say, I don't like your friend Trevor.

1788
01:18:44.430 --> 01:18:46.790
And then Rain says, I don't like him either.

1789
01:18:47.030 --> 01:18:48.690
And yet I really like him.

1790
01:18:49.150 --> 01:18:50.410
Oh my gosh.

1791
01:18:50.410 --> 01:18:52.050
I can't believe that was improvised.

1792
01:18:52.490 --> 01:18:53.370
And it made it in.

1793
01:18:53.370 --> 01:18:56.410
And I was so tickled when I saw it.

1794
01:18:57.170 --> 01:19:02.150
Because, you know, with me crying and Dwight puts his arm around me, they left the camera there for a bit.

1795
01:19:02.250 --> 01:19:02.490
Yeah.

1796
01:19:02.670 --> 01:19:05.010
And so I just said, I don't like your friend Trevor.

1797
01:19:05.390 --> 01:19:06.870
Such a funny thing to say.

1798
01:19:07.150 --> 01:19:09.710
And then Dwight has such a great response.

1799
01:19:09.810 --> 01:19:11.250
And then Rain's response is perfection.

1800
01:19:11.250 --> 01:19:14.090
We got a fan question from Jennifer C.

1801
01:19:14.210 --> 01:19:16.410
in Colorado Springs, Colorado, who said,

1802
01:19:16.610 --> 01:19:19.430
This is something I've wondered for years.

1803
01:19:19.990 --> 01:19:22.510
Does Mike sure hate jazz?

1804
01:19:23.110 --> 01:19:23.590
Oh.

1805
01:19:23.870 --> 01:19:28.630
I know he didn't write this episode, but this theme pops up in a lot of his projects.

1806
01:19:28.630 --> 01:19:31.330
And then Jennifer cites all these projects.

1807
01:19:31.590 --> 01:19:35.650
The Good Place, Season 1, Episode 9, someone says,

1808
01:19:35.810 --> 01:19:37.090
Ugh, I hate jazz.

1809
01:19:37.310 --> 01:19:38.470
It's like, we get it.

1810
01:19:38.650 --> 01:19:39.810
Wrap it up, Elton John.

1811
01:19:40.750 --> 01:19:46.410
In Parks and Recreation, in Season 5, Episode 12, someone says,

1812
01:19:46.870 --> 01:19:48.610
You'd really want to get to know the guy, right?

1813
01:19:48.770 --> 01:19:53.790
You'd want to know his personality, his favorite season of Friends, whether he hates jazz.

1814
01:19:53.990 --> 01:19:55.070
Hopefully he does.

1815
01:19:55.670 --> 01:19:57.270
There is a theme here.

1816
01:19:57.270 --> 01:19:59.730
Yes, and then jazz is stupid.

1817
01:20:00.090 --> 01:20:01.370
Just play the right notes.

1818
01:20:01.950 --> 01:20:05.750
Jennifer said, These are just three examples, but there are so many more.

1819
01:20:06.210 --> 01:20:07.350
Please explain.

1820
01:20:08.230 --> 01:20:12.670
So, one of my favorite things with our podcast is to reach out to people.

1821
01:20:12.930 --> 01:20:13.890
Please tell me.

1822
01:20:13.890 --> 01:20:16.090
You texted Mike Schur about this.

1823
01:20:16.230 --> 01:20:16.450
I did.

1824
01:20:16.670 --> 01:20:17.450
What did he say?

1825
01:20:17.650 --> 01:20:20.250
He wrote back, Ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha.

1826
01:20:21.790 --> 01:20:24.650
You can tell, Jennifer, I don't hate jazz.

1827
01:20:24.930 --> 01:20:27.130
I didn't even write all of those jokes.

1828
01:20:27.270 --> 01:20:40.450
But comedy writers like to pick on jazz because, like most art forms, it has a certain group of adherents who like to lecture you on why it's so amazing and so cool and so special.

1829
01:20:40.870 --> 01:20:46.570
Mike said, kind of like the Ryan Gosling character in La La Land where he explains jazz to John Legend.

1830
01:20:47.910 --> 01:20:56.730
Mike said, comedy writers in general have a disdain for anything that causes people to wax poetic or why you just don't get something.

1831
01:20:56.970 --> 01:20:59.590
And he said, jazz is one of those things.

1832
01:20:59.590 --> 01:21:01.710
What a perfect answer.

1833
01:21:01.950 --> 01:21:02.270
I know.

1834
01:21:02.590 --> 01:21:03.710
I love that.

1835
01:21:03.910 --> 01:21:09.630
Well, I love that Jennifer keyed in on this joke about jazz that keeps popping up.

1836
01:21:10.170 --> 01:21:19.830
You know, we also got quite a few people who wrote in and said, wait, so Angela likes babies who play jazz for posters, but not actual jazz?

1837
01:21:20.090 --> 01:21:21.630
Yes, I saw those.

1838
01:21:21.790 --> 01:21:30.170
I think it's not so much about babies playing jazz that Angela liked about the poster, but just the fact that they were dressed up in like a little fedora and they had instruments.

1839
01:21:30.170 --> 01:21:32.310
It's like when you dress up your pets.

1840
01:21:32.490 --> 01:21:36.930
I think Angela likes babies dressed as like adult things.

1841
01:21:37.230 --> 01:21:44.430
So like a favorite Halloween costume for Angela would be like a baby dressed like an old man.

1842
01:21:44.710 --> 01:21:48.850
Or a baby dressed like an old timey pilot.

1843
01:21:49.230 --> 01:21:49.750
Yes.

1844
01:21:49.990 --> 01:21:52.070
Or a race car driver.

1845
01:21:52.410 --> 01:21:52.930
That's right.

1846
01:21:53.150 --> 01:21:53.270
Yeah.

1847
01:21:53.530 --> 01:21:56.710
Kind of like how you like animals dressed as other animals.

1848
01:21:56.990 --> 01:21:57.410
Yes.

1849
01:21:57.410 --> 01:22:03.050
Like, I love nothing more than a bunny rabbit dressed as a bear stuffed animal.

1850
01:22:03.690 --> 01:22:04.150
Right.

1851
01:22:04.670 --> 01:22:06.670
This is such a weird thing that I like that.

1852
01:22:07.030 --> 01:22:08.710
What is that about?

1853
01:22:08.710 --> 01:22:09.510
I don't know.

1854
01:22:09.670 --> 01:22:10.210
I don't know.

1855
01:22:10.310 --> 01:22:11.330
We like what we like.

1856
01:22:11.410 --> 01:22:12.410
We like what we like.

1857
01:22:12.630 --> 01:22:16.670
Well, Jim is finally pulling into the parking lot now with Stanley and Phyllis.

1858
01:22:16.790 --> 01:22:17.930
They're asleep in the back seat.

1859
01:22:18.090 --> 01:22:19.070
They've eaten everything.

1860
01:22:19.150 --> 01:22:20.450
They've drank everything.

1861
01:22:21.230 --> 01:22:24.070
And Jim is just going to kind of tuck them in.

1862
01:22:24.210 --> 01:22:26.930
He's taking his coat, putting it over Phyllis like a blanket.

1863
01:22:27.490 --> 01:22:31.210
And she says, of course, we're going to cover for you, Jimmy.

1864
01:22:31.490 --> 01:22:32.690
We love you guys.

1865
01:22:32.930 --> 01:22:33.710
So sweet.

1866
01:22:33.930 --> 01:22:34.630
So sweet.

1867
01:22:34.630 --> 01:22:36.690
And she calls him Jimmy, which I love.

1868
01:22:36.870 --> 01:22:38.490
And then we're going to go to the warehouse.

1869
01:22:38.490 --> 01:22:39.430
And guess what?

1870
01:22:39.590 --> 01:22:41.590
There is paint on the wall.

1871
01:22:41.870 --> 01:22:42.690
It's happening.

1872
01:22:42.690 --> 01:22:50.170
And I have to tell you, there was a very sweet deleted scene between Pam and the senator that happened here.

1873
01:22:50.170 --> 01:22:55.790
So in the shooting draft, there was a scene where the senator shows up to the bullpen with coffee.

1874
01:22:56.050 --> 01:22:57.930
I guess he's just stopped by, right?

1875
01:22:58.530 --> 01:23:00.910
No one's in the bullpen except for Daryl.

1876
01:23:01.030 --> 01:23:02.370
And he says, hey, where is everyone?

1877
01:23:02.650 --> 01:23:05.370
And Daryl says, I think Angela's in the warehouse.

1878
01:23:05.370 --> 01:23:14.710
And the senator goes, okay, well, if you see Oscar, will you tell Oscar that I stopped by with coffee for Angela?

1879
01:23:15.410 --> 01:23:17.690
And Daryl gets this look like, what?

1880
01:23:17.990 --> 01:23:21.190
And Daryl's like, or I could just tell Angela herself.

1881
01:23:22.070 --> 01:23:24.970
And the senator goes, oh, yeah, I guess that would work, too.

1882
01:23:25.770 --> 01:23:28.990
And Daryl has this look like, what was that about?

1883
01:23:29.210 --> 01:23:32.250
And then the next scene is the senator walking into the warehouse.

1884
01:23:32.510 --> 01:23:33.630
And he sees Pam.

1885
01:23:33.750 --> 01:23:34.770
And they have this exchange.

1886
01:23:35.150 --> 01:23:40.190
And I really loved it because I feel like Pam has locked into something about herself.

1887
01:23:41.750 --> 01:23:42.510
Hello, Pam.

1888
01:23:43.130 --> 01:23:43.570
Senator.

1889
01:23:44.450 --> 01:23:46.110
I thought you were the office manager.

1890
01:23:47.250 --> 01:23:48.290
No, I'm an artiSt.

1891
01:23:49.490 --> 01:23:50.570
And I work in an office.

1892
01:23:52.550 --> 01:23:52.790
That.

1893
01:23:52.790 --> 01:23:53.970
I love that.

1894
01:23:54.450 --> 01:23:55.790
She just owns it.

1895
01:23:55.890 --> 01:23:56.810
She goes, I'm an artiSt.

1896
01:23:57.050 --> 01:23:58.650
I also do this other thing.

1897
01:23:58.750 --> 01:24:00.250
But that's where my heart is.

1898
01:24:00.430 --> 01:24:00.610
Yeah.

1899
01:24:00.990 --> 01:24:03.170
You know, we had a fan question from Bethany N.

1900
01:24:03.290 --> 01:24:06.730
in Australia who said, who actually painted the mural?

1901
01:24:06.990 --> 01:24:10.930
Well, Bethany, all the paint that you see on the wall in this episode was me.

1902
01:24:11.430 --> 01:24:12.690
I painted it.

1903
01:24:13.950 --> 01:24:17.790
Later, it was not me at all.

1904
01:24:17.790 --> 01:24:20.930
But I actually got to put paint up on the wall for this one.

1905
01:24:21.550 --> 01:24:23.730
Well, this episode is going to end with a tag.

1906
01:24:23.950 --> 01:24:26.070
Dwight and Angela are meeting with Toby.

1907
01:24:26.070 --> 01:24:34.430
Yeah, for some reason, Toby is who Dwight goes to for any kind of, like, questions about relationships or sex.

1908
01:24:34.970 --> 01:24:36.310
He goes to Toby.

1909
01:24:36.590 --> 01:24:37.150
Yes.

1910
01:24:37.250 --> 01:24:41.270
In the episode, Sexual Harassment, Dwight sits down with Toby.

1911
01:24:41.510 --> 01:24:41.730
Yeah.

1912
01:24:41.730 --> 01:24:42.130
Remember?

1913
01:24:42.370 --> 01:24:45.450
And he's asking about a woman's body.

1914
01:24:45.850 --> 01:24:46.510
A woman's pleasure areas.

1915
01:24:46.990 --> 01:24:47.230
Yes.

1916
01:24:47.230 --> 01:24:51.570
Yes, so for whatever reason, this is the go-to.

1917
01:24:52.050 --> 01:24:52.570
Toby.

1918
01:24:52.870 --> 01:24:53.210
Toby.

1919
01:24:53.530 --> 01:24:55.230
And he's always very kind.

1920
01:24:55.490 --> 01:24:56.170
And thoughtful.

1921
01:24:56.510 --> 01:24:56.910
Yes.

1922
01:24:57.610 --> 01:25:03.170
Well, Dwight and Angela have a lot of questions for Toby about, basically, Oscar and the senator.

1923
01:25:03.390 --> 01:25:03.650
Yes.

1924
01:25:03.650 --> 01:25:05.050
And how things are going down.

1925
01:25:05.290 --> 01:25:05.490
Right.

1926
01:25:06.110 --> 01:25:08.310
Brent shared with us something about this scene.

1927
01:25:08.430 --> 01:25:16.570
He said that it was Paul Lieberstein's idea that we speak in a whisper to make it seem that much more serious and ridiculous.

1928
01:25:17.210 --> 01:25:18.510
Yeah, that was a good choice.

1929
01:25:19.130 --> 01:25:21.110
Well, guys, there you have it.

1930
01:25:21.290 --> 01:25:22.390
The Target.

1931
01:25:22.990 --> 01:25:24.590
Gosh, I loved this episode.

1932
01:25:24.850 --> 01:25:27.270
I absolutely loved doing these scenes with Oscar.

1933
01:25:27.630 --> 01:25:31.530
You know, the show had a lot of love triangles, and this was one of my favorites.

1934
